From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: the dynarec code caches leave .text but stay in the module

Two separate facts about these three arrays, and conflating them cost a hardware round trip.

FACT ONE - THE SECTION NAME IS FATAL. core/oslib/virtmem.h puts each dynarec code cache in a
section literally named ".text". ps4/orbis-module.ld collects *(.text .text.*) into one output
section and an output section carries the UNION of its inputs' flags, so a writable array that is
merely NAMED .text stamps SHF_WRITE on the module's whole text:

    core/hw/sh4/dyna/driver.cpp.o   .text  WA  0xb00000   (SH4_TCB,    11 MiB)
    core/hw/arm7/arm7_rec.cpp.o     .text  WA  0x400000   (ARM7_TCB,    4 MiB)
    core/hw/aica/dsp_x64.cpp.o      .text  WA  0x008000   (CodeBuffer, 32 KiB)

They are WA, not WAX, which is why searching every object, archive and toolchain library for a
writable-EXECUTABLE input section found nothing. ld.lld will not keep a writable and a read-only
executable range in one PT_LOAD, so it split the text, and the second half began at whatever
offset the first ended on - 0x155cb38, not a multiple of this console's 16 KiB page:

    [rtld] ERROR scan_phdr:697: segment #1 of "(null)" is not page aligned.
    [rtld] ERROR self_load_shared_object:1757: found illegal segment header in
           /data/retroarch/cores/flycast_libretro.prx
    [rtld] syscall load_prx failed due to 0x00000008

FACT TWO - THE ARRAY ITSELF IS LOAD-BEARING. An earlier version of this patch declared
`static u8 *Name;` instead, copying the _WIN32/__APPLE__ arm at the top of the same chain. That
loads, and then dies: virtmem::prepare_jit_block falls through to a plain mmap, and this kernel
answers it wherever it likes - blocks measured at 0x899200000 and 0x8074d4000 against a module at
0x800870000. Xbyak emits rel32 for calls and jumps out of generated code, so more than 2 GiB
between the cache and the module's text is not a slowdown:

    libc++abi: terminating with uncaught exception of type Xbyak::Error: offset is too big

0.2 s after a module that finally loaded. So the array stays: inside the module's own image,
proximity is true by construction and cannot regress with the kernel's mood. Only the section name
goes, and an ordinary .bss array carries no SHF_EXECINSTR for anything to split on.

ALIGNMENT IS 16 KiB, NOT 4096, AND THAT IS THE PLATFORM'S PAGE. What the array needs instead of
a link-time flag is run-time permission, which core/linux/posix_vmem.cpp's __ORBIS__ arm takes
from sceKernelMprotect - a call that works in 16 KiB granules and rounds OUTWARDS. At alignas(4096)
that rounding would drag whichever neighbouring .bss shares the first and last granule into an
executable mapping. At alignas(0x4000) the promoted range is exactly the array; measured on the
built module, the three symbols sit at offsets 0x4000, 0x1004000 and 0x4000 of .bss sections that
are themselves 16384-aligned, with sizes 0xb00000, 0x400000 and 0x8000.

Result: three PT_LOADs, .text back to AX, and 15 MiB of PROGBITS zeroes gone from the shipped
file (39M -> 24M) because .bss is NOBITS.

diff --git a/core/oslib/virtmem.h b/core/oslib/virtmem.h
index 1a62db5..ce5c947 100644
--- a/core/oslib/virtmem.h
+++ b/core/oslib/virtmem.h
@@ -7,6 +7,41 @@
 #define DECLARE_CODE_CACHE(Name, Size) alignas(4096) static u8 Name[Size];
 #elif defined(__OpenBSD__)
 #define DECLARE_CODE_CACHE(Name, Size) alignas(4096) static u8 Name[Size] __attribute__((section(".openbsd.mutable")));
+#elif defined(__ORBIS__)
+// ⚠ THE ARRAY STAYS; ONLY THE SECTION NAME GOES. Two separable facts got conflated once here
+// already and the conflation cost a hardware round trip, so both are written down.
+//
+// FACT ONE - THE NAME IS FATAL. The __unix__ arm below puts each cache in a section literally
+// named ".text". ps4/orbis-module.ld collects *(.text .text.*) into one output section and an
+// output section's flags are the UNION of its inputs', so a writable array that is merely NAMED
+// .text stamps SHF_WRITE on the module's whole text. ld.lld will not keep a writable and a
+// read-only executable range in one PT_LOAD, so it splits the segment, and the second half starts
+// at whatever offset the first ended on - 0x155cb38, not a multiple of this console's 16 KiB
+// page. The loader checks:
+//
+//     [rtld] ERROR scan_phdr:697: segment #1 of "(null)" is not page aligned.
+//     [rtld] syscall load_prx failed due to 0x00000008
+//
+// FACT TWO - THE ARRAY IS LOAD-BEARING, AND A NULL POINTER HERE IS NOT THE ANSWER. Declaring
+// `static u8 *Name;` like the _WIN32/__APPLE__ arm above makes virtmem::prepare_jit_block fall
+// through to a plain mmap, and this kernel answers that wherever it likes - blocks have come back
+// at 0x899200000 and 0x8074d4000 while the module sat at 0x800870000. Xbyak writes rel32 for
+// calls and jumps out of generated code, so more than 2 GiB between the cache and the module's
+// text is not a slowdown, it is an abort, and it was measured as one:
+//
+//     libc++abi: terminating with uncaught exception of type Xbyak::Error: offset is too big
+//
+// A static array is inside the module's own image, so proximity is true by construction and
+// cannot regress. An ordinary .bss array carries no SHF_EXECINSTR and nothing splits.
+//
+// ⚠ alignas IS 16 KiB, NOT 4096, AND THAT IS THE PLATFORM'S PAGE. What the array then needs is
+// execute permission at run time, which core/linux/posix_vmem.cpp's __ORBIS__ arm gets through
+// sceKernelMprotect - and that call works in 16 KiB granules and rounds OUTWARDS. At 4096 the
+// rounding would drag whichever neighbouring .bss shares the first and last granule into an
+// executable mapping. Aligning the array to the real granule makes the promoted range exactly
+// the array. (The same 16 KiB granule is why the module's own .bss needs promoting to be
+// WRITABLE this far in at all - see the swanstation s_fast_map patch, same platform fact.)
+#define DECLARE_CODE_CACHE(Name, Size) alignas(0x4000) static u8 Name[Size];
 #elif defined(__unix__) || defined(__SWITCH__) || defined(__HAIKU__)
 #define DECLARE_CODE_CACHE(Name, Size) alignas(4096) static u8 Name[Size] __attribute__((section(".text")));
 #else
